8
00:00:54,480 --> 00:01:02,800
Until March 23, 2005, BP's Texas City, Texas refinery was one of America's most substantial energy producers.

9
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:11,780
At full capacity, it processed 11 million gallons of gasoline, or roughly 3% of the country's daily consumption.

10
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:21,480
But at 1.19 in the afternoon on that fateful day, a vapor cloud of combustible gas was spreading through the refinery.

11
00:01:21,481 --> 00:01:26,540
60 seconds later it found an ignition source

12
00:01:26,541 --> 00:01:37,440
America's third largest refinery suffered a major explosion the power from the blast wave

13
00:01:37,441 --> 00:01:42,260
set off a series of secondary explosions decimating a large portion of the facility

14
00:01:42,261 --> 00:01:50,700
the wave of the explosion was really something to behold a lot of windows broke within a radius of

15
00:01:50,701 --> 00:01:52,620
at least that's half a mile away.

16
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,580
The magnitude of this one was really unprecedented.

17
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:08,420
Fifteen people located in temporary work trailers were killed instantly.

18
00:02:09,780 --> 00:02:12,160
Over 170 others were counted as injured.

19
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:18,660
It became one of the worst petrochemical disasters in the United States in 15 years.

20
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:25,640
The epicenter of the explosion was the refinery's isomerization unit, or ISOM.

21
00:02:27,940 --> 00:02:33,020
A key component of the ISOM unit is a distillation tower, called a raffinate splitter.

22
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:41,500
The 164-foot distillation column separates out light hydrocarbons that are used to raise the octane level in gasoline.

23
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:47,320
The process starts with feeding hydrocarbons into the tower, where they're then heated.

24
00:02:47,321 --> 00:02:50,980
the light vapors rise to the top, where they cool and condense,

25
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:53,780
while the heavy liquid remains at the bottom.

26
00:02:55,480 --> 00:02:57,740
Although it stands 164 feet tall,

27
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:02,780
operating guidelines limit the maximum fluid level inside at 8 feet.

28
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:07,880
This allows for proper distillation and prevents overpressurizing the tower.

29
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:15,960
Yet, according to investigators from the United States Chemical Safety Board,

30
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:20,700
Workers regularly operated the tower outside safe limits during startup.

31
00:03:21,460 --> 00:03:28,620
Operators stated that it was typical practice to fill the tower to a higher level,

32
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:35,800
to allow for sufficient liquid and avoid a drop in the tower level when the bottom liquid was being heated up.

33
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:42,960
The operators were concerned that a low liquid level on the tower could kill the startup process.

34
00:03:42,961 --> 00:03:50,280
If the tower overpressurized, emergency relief valves would divert vapor and liquid to a

35
00:03:50,281 --> 00:03:53,540
disposal system called a blow-down drum.

36
00:03:53,541 --> 00:03:57,520
The drum had a vent stack that was open to the atmosphere.

37
00:03:57,521 --> 00:04:05,280
This resulted in a history of releases from the stack that formed vapor clouds at or near

38
00:04:05,281 --> 00:04:06,280
ground level.

39
00:04:06,281 --> 00:04:12,480
If you have a lot of that vapor vended to the atmosphere and there is a spark, that

40
00:04:12,481 --> 00:04:16,860
thing is really ripe for explosion.

41
00:04:16,861 --> 00:04:21,480
A safer alternative is a flare system, which burns off vapor and collects the flammable

42
00:04:21,481 --> 00:04:22,181
liquid.

43
00:04:22,182 --> 00:04:28,660
Yet despite ample profits, BP never fitted their blow-down drum with a flare.

44
00:04:28,661 --> 00:04:34,900
The events that led to the disaster commenced during the early morning hours on March 23rd,

45
00:04:34,901 --> 00:04:44,960
His workers began feeding liquid hydrocarbons into the ISOM's raffinate tower.

46
00:04:44,961 --> 00:04:52,340
The tower level began to build in the bottom ten feet of the 164 foot tall raffinate splitter

47
00:04:52,341 --> 00:04:53,640
tower.

48
00:04:53,641 --> 00:05:00,680
At approximately three in the morning, the high level alarm went off in the tower, indicating

49
00:05:00,681 --> 00:05:07,680
that the liquid level had reached 72% of the bottom 10 feet.

50
00:05:07,681 --> 00:05:14,680
As they had on previous occasions, workers acknowledged the alarm but continued to feed the tower.

51
00:05:14,681 --> 00:05:20,680
When the liquid level reached 9 feet, a secondary alarm failed and the level continued to rise.

52
00:05:20,681 --> 00:05:24,680
Since the tower's level transmitter only indicated height up to 10 feet,

53
00:05:24,681 --> 00:05:29,680
workers soon had no way to ascertain the rising level inside.

54
00:05:29,681 --> 00:05:38,040
When workers completed the initial startup process that evening, 13 feet of flammable liquid hydrocarbons filled the bottom of the tower.

55
00:05:43,160 --> 00:05:50,440
At 10 a.m., operators resumed the startup of the ISOM unit, sending additional liquid feed into the Raffinate Tower.

56
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:54,140
The level inside was rapidly rising.

57
00:05:54,141 --> 00:06:06,904
The level transmitter was malfunctioning, giving an accurate level, indicating that the level was dropping while the level was actually rising rapidly towards the top

58
00:06:06,905 --> 00:06:08,980
of the 164-foot-tall tower.

59
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:16,520
Pressure inside the column was growing, triggering a pressure alarm in the refinery's control room.

60
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:20,300
Operators quickly jumped into action.

61
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:23,960
They opened a vent valve on the raffinate tower to relieve pressure.

62
00:06:24,140 --> 00:06:27,140
and began sending the heated liquid to a storage tank.

63
00:06:29,560 --> 00:06:32,420
The removal of liquid from the bottom of the tower

64
00:06:32,421 --> 00:06:33,680
should have helped conditions.

65
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:37,900
However, the liquid leaving the bottom of the tower was hot.

66
00:06:38,260 --> 00:06:39,740
It was 304 degrees.

67
00:06:40,380 --> 00:06:43,360
There was a heat exchanger that exchanged the heat

68
00:06:43,361 --> 00:06:45,860
between the feed that was leaving the tower

69
00:06:45,861 --> 00:06:48,180
with the feed that was entering the tower.

70
00:06:48,181 --> 00:06:50,940
This caused the temperature of the liquid

71
00:06:50,941 --> 00:06:54,040
entering to jump 140 degrees.

72
00:06:55,640 --> 00:06:58,700
Liquid began to boil and expand inside,

73
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:02,760
eventually reaching the 164-foot ceiling

74
00:07:02,761 --> 00:07:05,040
and spilling into an overhead pipe.

75
00:07:06,840 --> 00:07:09,680
Within minutes, the pipe filled to capacity,

76
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:12,200
forcing open an emergency relief valve

77
00:07:12,201 --> 00:07:14,640
that diverted the highly flammable liquid

78
00:07:14,641 --> 00:07:16,160
to the refinery's blowdown drum.

79
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:19,980
The blowdown drum began to fill with liquid

80
00:07:19,981 --> 00:07:23,580
and in a matter of minutes, the blowdown drum had filled up

81
00:07:23,581 --> 00:07:28,520
and liquid erupted out the top of the blowdown stack in a geyser-like fashion.

82
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:32,080
A vapor cloud quickly engulfed the area.

83
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:38,900
Investigators believe that a running diesel truck

84
00:07:38,901 --> 00:07:42,460
located 25 feet from the blowdown drum ignited the gas.

85
00:07:49,980 --> 00:07:58,980
The refinery's importance to America's energy needs led the United States Chemical Safety Board

86
00:07:58,981 --> 00:08:00,568
to investigate the disaster.

87
00:08:00,569 --> 00:08:04,140
They uncovered a history of shocking safety lapses at the Texas

88
00:08:04,141 --> 00:08:05,175
City refinery.

89
00:08:05,176 --> 00:08:11,160
They had had four episodes where they'd actually released gasoline out of the top

90
00:08:11,161 --> 00:08:16,865
of the same equipment that eventually caused the explosion.

91
00:08:16,866 --> 00:08:19,960
In 2002, BP considered replacing

92
00:08:19,961 --> 00:08:24,360
replacing their outdated blow-down drum with a flare system.

93
00:08:24,361 --> 00:08:29,040
Unfortunately, it took a tragedy for the oil giant to finally take action.

94
00:08:29,041 --> 00:08:34,940
BP has announced and taken steps to eliminate all the blow-down drums

95
00:08:34,941 --> 00:08:40,840
that handle flammable hydrocarbons from the facility.

96
00:08:40,841 --> 00:08:48,920
OSHA fined BP a record $21 million for egregious willful violations at its Texas City refinery.

97
00:08:49,960 --> 00:08:55,700
Not only was the explosion in Texas City tragic, it may be symptomatic of a much larger issue.

98
00:08:56,400 --> 00:09:02,500
The whole refining industry in the United States is a disaster waiting to happen.

99
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:14,640
It's absolutely obsolete, outdated, old, bad from every conceivable way that proper engineering would like it to be.

100
00:09:14,641 --> 00:09:21,680
We haven't had a new refinery in the United States for 30 years now.

101
00:09:22,280 --> 00:09:27,240
Unfortunately, the Texas City disaster, bad as it was,

102
00:09:27,820 --> 00:09:29,980
it's probably not going to be the last one

103
00:09:29,981 --> 00:09:33,900
because all the conditions are there for it to happen again.

104
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:39,660
Since the tragedy, BP has announced that it will invest $1 billion

105
00:09:39,661 --> 00:09:42,500
to modify and improve its Texas City facility.

106
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:55,100
According to a study by the United Steelworkers Union, there are at least 33 refineries in

107
00:09:55,101 --> 00:10:01,580
the United States utilizing unflared vents to dispose of volatile hydrocarbon materials.

108
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:05,640
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

109
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,120
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

