What you won't find is any evidence

115
00:10:39,641 --> 00:10:42,640
of what was once the town of Times Beach.

116
00:10:42,641 --> 00:10:51,700
no the town didn't move it simply doesn't exist anymore times beach was founded in 1925 by the

117
00:10:51,701 --> 00:10:58,060
st louis star times newspaper if you bought a lot in times beach you got six months subscription to

118
00:10:58,061 --> 00:11:04,240
the newspaper it was named times after the newspaper and beach because of the merrimack

119
00:11:04,241 --> 00:11:11,480
River which ran alongside the community in its first incarnation times beach was

120
00:11:11,481 --> 00:11:16,680
modeled as a resort town but by the 1970s the city had transformed into a

121
00:11:16,681 --> 00:11:23,080
lower middle-class suburb of roughly 2000 the town struggled financially and

122
00:11:23,081 --> 00:11:29,360
could never raise enough money to pave its 23 miles of dirt streets and roads

123
00:11:29,361 --> 00:11:32,360
Plagued by dust in the summer months,

124
00:11:32,361 --> 00:11:35,360
residents procured the services of a waste oil company

125
00:11:35,361 --> 00:11:37,360
to take care of the problem.

126
00:11:37,361 --> 00:11:38,360
The neighbors would get together,

127
00:11:38,361 --> 00:11:40,360
and they would have the streets sprayed

128
00:11:40,361 --> 00:11:43,360
just in their area to suppress the dust.

129
00:11:43,361 --> 00:11:46,360
And then in 1972, the city contracted with Russell Bliss

130
00:11:46,361 --> 00:11:51,360
to spray at will, and he did that in 72 and 73.

131
00:11:51,361 --> 00:11:54,360
A lot of little suburbs would hire me to oil the roads.

132
00:11:54,361 --> 00:11:58,360
I do a lot of little communities.

133
00:11:58,361 --> 00:12:04,720
Times Beach was pretty big and it would take a couple loads to do the whole town

134
00:12:04,721 --> 00:12:09,400
but with all the wear and tear I'd have to do it maybe once every couple weeks

135
00:12:09,401 --> 00:12:16,520
or once a month but bliss didn't use just oil for dust control during the

136
00:12:16,521 --> 00:12:20,740
years he sprayed the roads of Times Beach he used a mixture of industrial

137
00:12:20,741 --> 00:12:26,320
waste that he obtained from various suppliers including the Nopako

138
00:12:26,321 --> 00:12:28,446
chemical company in Verona Missouri.

139
00:12:28,447 --> 00:12:31,340
Napaco shared the facility in Verona with another

140
00:12:31,341 --> 00:12:33,654
company named Hoffman Taft.

141
00:12:33,655 --> 00:12:40,340
Hoffman Taft produced the chemical 245T while Napaco produced hexachlorophene.

142
00:12:40,341 --> 00:12:49,080
245T is the short name for a pesticide which was combined with another pesticide 2,4-D

143
00:12:49,081 --> 00:12:53,427
and together formed the compound which is commonly known as Agent Orange.

144
00:12:53,428 --> 00:12:54,320
Hexachlorophene

145
00:12:54,321 --> 00:12:56,660
is an antibacterial agent.

146
00:12:56,661 --> 00:12:59,420
It was used until it was banned in soaps,

147
00:12:59,421 --> 00:13:02,360
toothpaste, and other medical supplies.

148
00:13:02,361 --> 00:13:05,000
Both of these chemical manufacturing processes

149
00:13:05,001 --> 00:13:09,360
created a waste stream which was laden with dioxin.

150
00:13:11,680 --> 00:13:14,400
Dioxin is a known carcinogen.

151
00:13:14,401 --> 00:13:18,720
But in 1971, little was known about its harmful effects,

152
00:13:18,721 --> 00:13:20,380
and there were no federal laws in place

153
00:13:20,381 --> 00:13:23,580
regarding how to dispose of it or any industrial waste.

154
00:13:23,581 --> 00:13:24,840
They could have told me it's cream cheese,

155
00:13:24,841 --> 00:13:26,680
and I wouldn't know the difference between that and dioxin.

156
00:13:26,681 --> 00:13:30,720
I wouldn't know what dioxin really was.

157
00:13:31,880 --> 00:13:34,800
He hauled tanker trucks of this material

158
00:13:34,801 --> 00:13:37,900
where it was mixed in large holding tanks.

159
00:13:38,520 --> 00:13:41,060
He then would draw down off of this

160
00:13:41,061 --> 00:13:45,100
and use the resulting mixture to spray on unpaved roads,

161
00:13:45,560 --> 00:13:47,920
parking lots, and in some cases horse arenas

162
00:13:47,921 --> 00:13:49,420
in order to control dust.

163
00:13:49,421 --> 00:13:58,360
1971 brought the first sign that bliss's brew was toxic when a number of horses died at an arena he

164
00:13:58,361 --> 00:14:06,160
sprayed the owners of the arena contacted the state health department to seek their help in

165
00:14:06,161 --> 00:14:12,580
trying to identify the toxic agent the Missouri Department of Health then solicited assistance

166
00:14:12,581 --> 00:14:15,420
from the U.S. Centers for Disease Control.

167
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:21,680
In 1974, the CDC identified dioxin

168
00:14:21,681 --> 00:14:24,520
as the toxic agent responsible for the horse deaths.

169
00:14:25,680 --> 00:14:28,480
That drew in officials from the Environmental Protection Agency,

170
00:14:28,840 --> 00:14:31,700
which eventually traced the dioxin to the Nopako facility

171
00:14:31,701 --> 00:14:35,000
in Verona, Missouri, and to Russell Bliss.

172
00:14:36,420 --> 00:14:40,260
We had a list of places where Russell Bliss

173
00:14:40,261 --> 00:14:44,671
was alleged to have sprayed dioxin contaminated waste oil.

174
00:14:44,672 --> 00:14:47,180
EPA was able to identify a number

175
00:14:47,181 --> 00:14:55,140
of locations to sample, and in fact we did identify Times Beach as one of the locations.

176
00:14:55,141 --> 00:14:58,960
Residents of Times Beach were dismayed to learn they'd been living on dioxin contaminated

177
00:14:58,961 --> 00:15:01,740
soil for over a decade.

178
00:15:01,741 --> 00:15:04,140
I had no idea what dioxin was.

179
00:15:04,141 --> 00:15:08,940
I had no idea what it was used for, how it was manufactured.

180
00:15:08,941 --> 00:15:12,640
My name is Russell Martin Bliss.

181
00:15:12,641 --> 00:15:15,980
The state made it big to boot, to be out of it.

182
00:15:15,981 --> 00:15:20,060
They said it was the most deadly poisonous known to man.

183
00:15:20,061 --> 00:15:26,740
Well, I went before hearing of a bunch of political people in Jefferson City, and I took my finger

184
00:15:26,741 --> 00:15:30,540
to it and tasted it, and it never affected me.

185
00:15:30,541 --> 00:15:33,860
I wasn't told what was in the oil.

186
00:15:33,861 --> 00:15:36,940
I wouldn't have known what it was if they had told me what it was.

187
00:15:36,941 --> 00:15:41,920
you want the truth you could tell me it was some kind of a new jelly and I put it on toast and eat

188
00:15:41,921 --> 00:15:47,920
it but I didn't know what docks and was I swear to all of you I had no idea this material was bad

189
00:15:47,921 --> 00:15:58,900
in November of 1982 the EPA took soil samples from Times Beach to ascertain the severity of

190
00:15:58,901 --> 00:16:06,800
the dioxin contamination on December 23rd just two days before Christmas their worst fears were

191
00:16:06,801 --> 00:16:12,740
realized. EPA was receiving the initial results from the lab sampling that

192
00:16:12,741 --> 00:16:17,480
indicated dioxin concentrations as high as 100 parts per billion existed on the

193
00:16:17,481 --> 00:16:26,800
roadways. Dioxin is considered hazardous to humans at only one part per billion.

194
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:32,320
Times Beach was abandoned.

195
00:16:32,321 --> 00:16:36,920
With their city deemed unlivable, residents lobbied for a buyout.

196
00:16:37,540 --> 00:16:41,820
They knew that there was no technology available to clean up the community,

197
00:16:42,420 --> 00:16:46,160
and they knew they couldn't move back because it wasn't safe.

198
00:16:48,280 --> 00:16:52,740
In 1983, the estranged residents of Times Beach got their wish.

199
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:56,740
The federal government's Superfund program,

200
00:16:56,741 --> 00:16:59,460
which provides funds for hazardous waste sites,

201
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:01,500
paid residents for their property.

202
00:17:02,320 --> 00:17:11,460
Soon after, the state of Missouri disincorporated the city.

203
00:17:11,461 --> 00:17:18,920
For over a decade, the hazardous waste site was gated off and patrolled 24 hours a day.

204
00:17:18,921 --> 00:17:24,120
The once thriving community was transformed into a ghost town.

205
00:17:24,121 --> 00:17:38,121
Finally, in 1997, after the completion of numerous investigations and lawsuits, the EPA

206
00:17:38,880 --> 00:17:42,800
embarked on a massive cleanup effort.

207
00:17:42,801 --> 00:17:48,000
All standing structures were bulldozed and placed inside a landfill the size of four football

208
00:17:48,001 --> 00:17:52,780
fields.

209
00:17:52,781 --> 00:17:58,880
The EPA then brought in a massive incinerator to purge the dioxin from the Times Beach soil.

210
00:17:58,881 --> 00:18:03,840
That material was excavated and transported by covered trucks and then it would go through

211
00:18:03,841 --> 00:18:09,740
a double airlock into a material handling facility where the trucks were dumped and

212
00:18:09,741 --> 00:18:12,620
managed under very controlled conditions.

213
00:18:12,621 --> 00:18:19,040
The material would then be augured in a closed system up into the entry to the incinerator.

214
00:18:19,041 --> 00:18:26,080
The gases would be evaporated at a temperature of 1,700 degrees, and then the gases themselves,

215
00:18:26,081 --> 00:18:32,220
which contained the dioxin molecules, would flow into a secondary combustion chamber,

216
00:18:32,221 --> 00:18:40,780
which would actually destroy the dioxin molecules at a temperature of more than 2,100 degrees.

217
00:18:40,781 --> 00:18:46,280
Over $200 million later, the cleanup project came to an end.

218
00:18:46,281 --> 00:18:54,160
In 1999, Times Beach was reborn as a state park.

219
00:18:55,240 --> 00:18:57,460
Today, wildlife flourishes here,

220
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:01,900
showing no ill effects from the deadly dioxin that once permeated the soil.

221
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:06,560
But some of the town's former residents feel they aren't as fortunate.

222
00:19:06,880 --> 00:19:09,420
I had eight brothers and one sister,

223
00:19:09,620 --> 00:19:14,840
and what we had as children growing up was bruises, scratches, a few stitches,

224
00:19:14,841 --> 00:19:22,020
maybe a broken arm nothing like my family every member of my family was ill with some something

225
00:19:22,021 --> 00:19:30,760
different so you have to believe that it's because of the exposure to the chemicals I felt there was

226
00:19:30,761 --> 00:19:36,620
nothing wrong with the oil I didn't even think about anything being wrong with the oil Russell

227
00:19:36,621 --> 00:19:41,280
Bliss was never found guilty of any criminal behavior resulting from the dioxin contamination

228
00:19:41,281 --> 00:19:49,520
Yet, many still see him as the man that killed the city of Times Beach.

229
00:19:49,521 --> 00:19:54,680
When you say Deoxin or Times Beach, you don't think about Nopako, you don't think about

230
00:19:54,681 --> 00:20:02,160
all the other companies that were involved in it, you think of one thing, Russell Bliss.

231
00:20:02,161 --> 00:20:08,800
But to this day I will say this, I didn't generate it, I didn't make it, I hauled it

232
00:20:08,801 --> 00:20:10,680
from there and put it over there.

233
00:20:10,681 --> 00:20:13,280
I was a transporter. No more, no less.

234
00:20:16,700 --> 00:20:18,880
Times Beach may no longer exist,

235
00:20:19,480 --> 00:20:23,860
but the physical and emotional scars of those that lived through its demise endure.

236
00:20:28,080 --> 00:20:31,040
According to the EPA, uncontrolled burning,

237
00:20:31,041 --> 00:20:33,780
especially of household products that contain chlorine,

238
00:20:34,360 --> 00:20:38,840
accounts for 57% of all dioxin releases into the environment.

239
00:20:38,841 --> 00:20:42,300
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

240
00:20:44,460 --> 00:20:48,840
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

241
00:20:52,440 --> 00:20:54,860
September 11, 2001.

242
00:20:56,420 --> 00:21:00,080
Al-Qaeda operatives hijack and crash four U.S. commercial planes,

243
00:21:01,020 --> 00:21:04,600
bringing down the World Trade Center towers and damaging the Pentagon.

244
00:21:04,601 --> 00:21:11,600
on. Just two months later, it appears that the unthinkable happens again.

245
00:21:15,600 --> 00:21:17,191
November 12th, 2001.

246
00:21:17,192 --> 00:21:22,600
American Airlines Flight 587 mysteriously falls from the sky shortly

247
00:21:23,200 --> 00:21:25,829
after leaving JFK Airport.

248
00:21:25,830 --> 00:21:32,200
It crashes into a residential neighborhood in Queens, New York.

249
00:21:32,201 --> 00:21:35,700
260 passengers and five people on the ground are killed.

250
00:21:36,400 --> 00:21:39,960
The FBI and investigators from the National Transportation Safety Board

251
00:21:39,961 --> 00:21:42,460
quickly mobilize to determine the cause.

252
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:47,880
We assembled a team of probably 15 people to immediately fly up to New York

253
00:21:47,881 --> 00:21:49,180
and begin the investigation.

254
00:21:49,640 --> 00:21:54,000
It was impossible, really impossible not to, at least at some level,

255
00:21:54,660 --> 00:21:59,340
assume that it might not have been an accident and it might have been a terrorist act.

256
00:22:00,240 --> 00:22:04,120
The FBI found no obvious evidence of terrorism at the crash site.

257
00:22:05,100 --> 00:22:10,760
But investigators for the NTSB uncovered a vital clue that suggested a mechanical failure.

258
00:22:14,800 --> 00:22:20,500
The vertical stabilizer was located in the water in Jamaica Bay, about a mile from the accident site.

259
00:22:21,220 --> 00:22:26,780
We knew that some part of the aircraft's structure had failed, and our big job was to figure out why.

260
00:22:26,781 --> 00:22:35,020
to lose a vertical fin is a pretty serious situation it makes the airplane very difficult

261
00:22:35,021 --> 00:22:40,660
to control if not impossible in this case apparently impossible an aircraft's vertical

262
00:22:40,661 --> 00:22:47,360
stabilizer which includes the rudder provides lateral stability during flight take it away

263
00:22:47,361 --> 00:22:54,680
and the airplane will wallow from side to side lose altitude and crash

264
00:22:58,120 --> 00:23:04,860
flight 587 was on an Airbus a300 600 aircraft the vertical stabilizer had a

265
00:23:04,861 --> 00:23:10,440
unique quality it was manufactured from composite material layers of carbon

266
00:23:10,441 --> 00:23:15,360
fiber that provide a lightweight alternative to aluminum

267
00:23:15,361 --> 00:23:21,300
And we saw the six lugs made up of composite material broken.

268
00:23:21,620 --> 00:23:23,980
The rest of the fin, the composite fin was in the bay.

269
00:23:24,460 --> 00:23:29,020
The lugs that were supposed to hold it on were there, but they were cleanly broken off.

270
00:23:34,220 --> 00:23:37,940
The investigation team soon made an equally important discovery.

271
00:23:39,800 --> 00:23:44,540
They recovered Flight 587's flight data recorder and its cockpit voice recorder.

272
00:23:45,360 --> 00:23:55,860
With these key pieces of evidence, NTSB investigators embarked on the lengthy process of determining what caused the vertical stabilizer of Flight 587 to fracture.

273
00:23:57,220 --> 00:24:01,440
They started by scrutinizing the structural integrity of its composite material.

274
00:24:02,120 --> 00:24:08,500
We tested it down to the micro level, so to speak, by pulling bits of it apart, make sure it was strong enough.

275
00:24:08,501 --> 00:24:15,320
We concluded that the design of the structure was satisfactory,

276
00:24:15,720 --> 00:24:21,560
and in fact the strength of the material was stronger than it was designed to be.

277
00:24:23,360 --> 00:24:27,260
Investigators then turned to analyzing Flight 587's voice recorder,

278
00:24:28,080 --> 00:24:32,400
a key piece of evidence, allowing them to eavesdrop on the pilot's final words.

279
00:24:32,401 --> 00:24:40,500
when we listen to the voice recorder we heard the captain mention wake turbulence then we went back

280
00:24:40,501 --> 00:24:46,680
to the recorded radar data factored in the winds and proved that indeed he did run through this

281
00:24:46,681 --> 00:24:54,180
wake turbulence wake turbulence is rough air that forms from the wingtips of airplanes

282
00:24:54,181 --> 00:25:02,040
studies show that wake turbulence resembles a horizontal tornado when encountered by another

283
00:25:02,041 --> 00:25:12,360
aircraft the vortex causes the wings of that aircraft to dip down or bank it was immediately

284
00:25:12,361 --> 00:25:17,400
evident that wake turbulence in itself couldn't have caused this accident for one thing there

285
00:25:17,401 --> 00:25:21,620
was five miles of separation for another thing the airplanes were already well established in

286
00:25:21,621 --> 00:25:25,380
the climb and that means that they were going at a pretty good speed the

287
00:25:25,381 --> 00:25:29,860
strength of turbulence diminishes as speed increases and so it just didn't

288
00:25:29,861 --> 00:25:35,580
make any sense having ruled out wake turbulence and the vertical stabilizers

289
00:25:35,581 --> 00:25:40,520
composite material the investigation shifted again

290
00:25:40,521 --> 00:25:45,500
NTSB officials conducted interviews to determine the competency of the co-pilot

291
00:25:45,501 --> 00:25:50,060
who was commanding the aircraft when it crashed this one gentleman was a captain

292
00:25:50,061 --> 00:25:55,200
in a Boeing 727 when the accident first officer was his first officer.

293
00:25:56,940 --> 00:26:01,860
They ran into a bit of vague turbulence, and the first officer reacted strangely.

294
00:26:02,300 --> 00:26:05,580
In a sense, he activated the rudders back and forth several times,

295
00:26:05,900 --> 00:26:07,800
causing the aircraft to yaw quite heavily.

296
00:26:15,500 --> 00:26:18,960
Fully deflecting the rudder back and forth as the co-pilot had done,

297
00:26:18,961 --> 00:26:21,860
stood out as a red flag to investigators.

298
00:26:23,060 --> 00:26:25,940
An analysis of Flight 587's data recorder

299
00:26:25,941 --> 00:26:30,060
showed that the co-pilot had once again repeated this abnormal maneuver.

300
00:26:32,420 --> 00:26:35,440
He jumped the gun on responding,

301
00:26:35,700 --> 00:26:38,720
and that as the airplane swung one way,

302
00:26:39,020 --> 00:26:41,880
he came in with opposite rudder,

303
00:26:42,120 --> 00:26:46,500
and that the airplane then swung back and overshot in the other direction

304
00:26:46,501 --> 00:26:48,620
because he had put in too much rudder control.

305
00:26:48,960 --> 00:26:53,340
And so he then went to the other rudder pedal and tried to correct this.

306
00:26:55,260 --> 00:26:59,740
This led to a shocking revelation, dispelling any theory of a terrorist plot,

307
00:27:00,320 --> 00:27:06,220
that a pilot could inadvertently cause severe structural damage to an airplane at maneuvering speed.

308
00:27:09,200 --> 00:27:14,620
Maneuvering speed is a speed below which, in the general understanding of pilots,

309
00:27:14,621 --> 00:27:20,460
any deflection of the controls can be made with any speed, any violence.

310
00:27:20,900 --> 00:27:25,080
The plane can be maneuvered in any way you choose without endangering the structure.

311
00:27:25,400 --> 00:27:30,580
However, it turns out that there was some fine print in the certification of airplanes.

312
00:27:30,581 --> 00:27:33,780
And one of the provisions of the fine print was essentially

313
00:27:33,781 --> 00:27:39,760
that you couldn't flap the rudder back and forth the way this pilot allegedly had done.

314
00:27:39,761 --> 00:27:46,560
The A300-600 was somewhat susceptible to this type of anomaly.

315
00:27:46,561 --> 00:27:52,180
At the speed the aircraft was traveling, about 250 knots, it only took about one and a quarter

316
00:27:52,181 --> 00:27:58,760
inches of rudder travel to move the rudder all the way to 9.3 degrees, either way, and

317
00:27:58,761 --> 00:28:03,140
it only took about 32 pounds of rudder pedal force.

318
00:28:03,141 --> 00:28:07,540
And it takes 20 pounds just to get the rudder to move, so there's not a lot of margin there

319
00:28:07,541 --> 00:28:08,540
at all.

320
00:28:08,541 --> 00:28:11,220
Good morning, Madam Chairman and members of the board.

321
00:28:12,480 --> 00:28:21,080
The final report issued by the NTSB fell short of citing the Airbus A300-600 rudder sensitivity as a design flaw.

322
00:28:21,760 --> 00:28:25,940
But it did recommend that future Airbus planes incorporate a fail-safe mechanism

323
00:28:25,941 --> 00:28:29,420
that would prevent a pilot from critically damaging the aircraft.

324
00:28:30,700 --> 00:28:35,400
As of 2006, Airbus has yet to modify its A300-600.

325
00:28:36,380 --> 00:28:39,520
Nevertheless, since the crash of Flight 587,

326
00:28:40,120 --> 00:28:42,600
the Airbus rudder system has performed flawlessly.

327
00:28:43,220 --> 00:28:45,160
This type of accident has never happened before.

328
00:28:45,680 --> 00:28:47,260
It hasn't happened since the accident,

329
00:28:47,860 --> 00:28:50,780
and we would hope it would never happen again.

330
00:28:55,140 --> 00:28:58,120
Flight 587 is the second American Airlines jet

331
00:28:58,121 --> 00:29:00,540
to crash shortly after taking off from JFK.

332
00:29:01,100 --> 00:29:03,260
It's also the second to crash from rudder failure.

333
00:29:03,261 --> 00:29:12,660
In 1962, American Airlines Flight 1 crashed into Jamaica Bay after a wiring failure caused its rudder to deflect uncontrollably.

334
00:29:13,520 --> 00:29:16,560
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

335
00:29:18,160 --> 00:29:22,560
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

336
00:29:22,561 --> 00:29:36,380
On May 14, 1973, a Saturn V rocket blasted off from Kennedy Space Center to thunderous

337
00:29:36,381 --> 00:29:43,220
applause.

338
00:29:43,221 --> 00:29:47,620
Attached to the rocket was precious cargo.

339
00:29:47,621 --> 00:29:54,620
The $2.5 billion Skylab, America's first space station.

340
00:29:55,260 --> 00:29:58,520
But seconds after takeoff, disaster struck.

341
00:29:58,521 --> 00:30:03,080
A gap of less than half a centimeter between Skylab's thermal heat shield and the body

342
00:30:03,081 --> 00:30:09,060
of the space station allowed a pocket of air to form underneath.

343
00:30:09,061 --> 00:30:13,400
The resulting pressure snapped the three aluminum tension straps that held the shield of the

344
00:30:13,401 --> 00:30:17,560
spacecraft causing it to rip away

345
00:30:20,080 --> 00:30:25,440
soon after Skylab reached its intended orbit mission control realized the

346
00:30:25,441 --> 00:30:30,440
space station was critically damaged the temperatures inside the workshop began

347
00:30:30,441 --> 00:30:36,000
to go up and it didn't take them too long to figure out that they had lost the

348
00:30:36,001 --> 00:30:42,200
heat shield during launch and this heat shield ripped around to solar panel

349
00:30:42,201 --> 00:30:45,420
Number one, it took it off the vehicle, too.

350
00:30:45,820 --> 00:30:51,580
But when it got to the second solar panel, it ripped around it, fortunately, and left it intact.

351
00:30:51,960 --> 00:30:57,580
But a piece of aluminum strap from the heat shield riveted itself into the solar panel cover

352
00:30:57,581 --> 00:31:00,440
and kept it from deploying more than about a foot.

353
00:31:03,180 --> 00:31:06,360
If Skylab's second main solar panel were set free,

354
00:31:06,960 --> 00:31:10,640
the space station could generate enough electricity to perform its mission.

355
00:31:12,200 --> 00:31:17,480
But without a thermal heat shield, the temperatures inside Skylab would make it unlivable.

356
00:31:18,380 --> 00:31:22,680
If Skylab were lost, the future of the American space program would be in jeopardy.

357
00:31:23,520 --> 00:31:30,380
The crew of Skylab 2, who were to occupy the space station the following day, had their mission postponed indefinitely.

358
00:31:30,860 --> 00:31:34,980
There were questions that had to have answers in order for us to go.

359
00:31:34,981 --> 00:31:39,600
One was, how can we get some kind of a sunshade over the sunny side of Skylab

360
00:31:39,601 --> 00:31:43,160
to cool the temperatures so that it's livable in the first place?

361
00:31:43,520 --> 00:31:46,780
The second one is, how do we get that solar panel freed up?

362
00:31:48,240 --> 00:31:52,720
Devising a plan for how the astronauts could attach the replacement heat shield to the space station

363
00:31:52,721 --> 00:31:54,360
would be the biggest hurdle.

364
00:31:56,860 --> 00:32:01,960
Several NASA departments worked on designs that involved the astronauts replacing Skylab's heat shield

365
00:32:01,961 --> 00:32:03,860
while performing a spacewalk.

366
00:32:04,980 --> 00:32:13,460
But the tethered constraints necessary for a spacewalk would make it difficult to deploy

367
00:32:13,461 --> 00:32:16,380
and anchor a replacement thermal shield.

368
00:32:16,381 --> 00:32:21,620
NASA engineer Jack Kinsler used some inside information to develop a different strategy.

369
00:32:21,621 --> 00:32:27,280
There was a trainer equivalent to it right in the buildings where I worked.

370
00:32:27,281 --> 00:32:32,780
So I thought, why don't I walk over to the building and look around.

371
00:32:32,781 --> 00:32:39,140
There was a camera that was designed on the outboard end of the Skylab, and I saw that

372
00:32:39,141 --> 00:32:44,420
camera port, and gosh, I knew it was near the damaged area.

373
00:32:44,421 --> 00:32:50,000
So my thoughts just clicked over, and I bet I can put something through that 8-inch port

374
00:32:50,001 --> 00:32:58,880
that can expand and get big enough to lay out a very repairable piece of material.

375
00:32:58,881 --> 00:33:00,740
Kinsler ran with his idea.

376
00:33:00,741 --> 00:33:05,640
In hours, engineers stitched together a 24-square-foot piece of mylar

377
00:33:05,641 --> 00:33:10,180
and placed it inside a four-foot-long, eight-inch-wide aluminum box

378
00:33:10,181 --> 00:33:14,840
that would fit Skylab's scientific airlock.

379
00:33:14,841 --> 00:33:18,580
Kinsler then demonstrated his theory to NASA chiefs.

380
00:33:18,581 --> 00:33:23,500
Now, there's at least a dozen people there, all the top people from NASA,

381
00:33:23,501 --> 00:33:25,700
that could get close to it.

382
00:33:25,701 --> 00:33:28,740
And they were there watching all this.

383
00:33:28,741 --> 00:33:31,980
So the instant that thing splayed out on the floor,

384
00:33:32,480 --> 00:33:34,160
I had them all walk over to me and say,

385
00:33:34,420 --> 00:33:36,100
you've got it. That's it.

386
00:33:38,220 --> 00:33:39,660
With NASA's approval,

387
00:33:40,220 --> 00:33:43,340
Kinsler's group started building Skylab's replacement sunshade.

388
00:33:45,040 --> 00:33:47,400
After the fabric for the parasol was complete,

389
00:33:48,060 --> 00:33:50,240
Kinsler worked on designing a deployment mechanism

390
00:33:50,241 --> 00:33:53,660
that would allow his parasol to open up in a uniform fashion,

391
00:33:54,360 --> 00:33:55,200
much like an umbrella.

392
00:33:55,201 --> 00:34:00,580
I had devised a four-way spring.

393
00:34:00,720 --> 00:34:04,300
It had four coil springs all set in a little container.

394
00:34:04,780 --> 00:34:09,420
They were timed in such a way that as soon as they emerged,

395
00:34:09,820 --> 00:34:13,920
they would all spring simultaneously, and they opened up all four sides.

396
00:34:16,220 --> 00:34:19,520
Just nine days after Skylab reached its planned orbit,

397
00:34:20,420 --> 00:34:22,660
Kinsler's sunshade was rushed to Cape Canaveral

398
00:34:22,661 --> 00:34:27,300
and placed inside a Saturn IV-B rocket designated Skylab 2.

399
00:34:29,100 --> 00:34:33,940
Its crew, Joe Kerwin, Paul Weitz, and Pete Conrad,

400
00:34:34,680 --> 00:34:37,140
would attempt to save the crippled space station.

401
00:34:55,794 --> 00:35:02,060
On May 25th, the astronauts of Skylab 2 docked with the space station and felt the impact

402
00:35:02,061 --> 00:35:04,260
of Skylab's lost heat shield firsthand.

403
00:35:04,261 --> 00:35:08,760
The docking adapter and the airlock were not extremely hot

404
00:35:08,761 --> 00:35:10,980
because they hadn't lost their heat shield.

405
00:35:11,580 --> 00:35:14,960
The workshop where we are now had lost its heat shield,

406
00:35:15,400 --> 00:35:17,240
and it was about 130 degrees.

407
00:35:17,600 --> 00:35:18,440
It was very hot.

408
00:35:18,500 --> 00:35:21,120
It was like being in the engine room of an old Navy ship.

409
00:35:22,500 --> 00:35:24,920
Pete Conrad and Paul came back down

410
00:35:24,921 --> 00:35:28,820
with the container containing Jack Kinsler's parasol.

411
00:35:29,440 --> 00:35:31,860
What Pete and Paul did was to take the container,

412
00:35:31,861 --> 00:35:36,300
stick it into the sunny side airlock and deploy the parasol through it.

413
00:35:39,360 --> 00:35:46,060
Per Kinsler's design, Weitz and Conrad connected a series of six 32-inch long interlocking rods.

414
00:35:47,440 --> 00:35:51,240
They then pushed the assemblage through an opening in the back of the metal canister,

415
00:35:51,840 --> 00:35:53,120
attaching it to the parasol.

416
00:35:54,740 --> 00:36:00,200
The astronauts then opened the airlock and used the pushrod to move the parasol out into space.

417
00:36:00,201 --> 00:36:07,200
Conrad then pressed a release knob that activated Kinsler's spring mechanism, forcing the sunshade open.

418
00:36:07,201 --> 00:36:14,200
The temperature inside Skylab eventually stabilized at 80 degrees.

419
00:36:14,201 --> 00:36:20,200
Days later, the astronauts performed a spacewalk and freed the jammed solar panel.

420
00:36:20,201 --> 00:36:29,200
With only one main solar panel and a makeshift heat shield, Skylab looked worse for wear, but the space station worked.

421
00:36:29,201 --> 00:36:30,900
the space station worked.

422
00:36:30,901 --> 00:36:36,460
Apollo 13 was the mission where the crew was saved by flight control.

423
00:36:36,461 --> 00:36:41,440
Skylab was the mission that was saved by the engineering community.

424
00:36:41,441 --> 00:36:46,600
It was a great ten days watching everybody do their job.

425
00:36:46,601 --> 00:36:54,460
Astronauts Joe Kerwin, Pete Conrad and Paul Weitz lived on Skylab for a record 28 days.

426
00:36:54,461 --> 00:37:00,260
Over the next 12 months, two additional manned missions to the space station followed.

427
00:37:00,261 --> 00:37:13,320
In 1974, the hugely successful Skylab program came to an end.

428
00:37:13,321 --> 00:37:19,480
On July 11, 1979, Skylab returned to Earth in an uncontrolled trajectory.

429
00:37:19,481 --> 00:37:23,480
crashed in a remote area of Western Australia, killing one cow.

430
00:37:24,480 --> 00:37:27,620
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

431
00:37:28,320 --> 00:37:32,460
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

432
00:37:39,460 --> 00:37:46,220
On June 24, 2005, fire erupted at the Praxair Gas Company in St. Louis, Missouri.

433
00:37:46,221 --> 00:37:53,220
the blaze quickly progressed into a raging inferno sending pressurized

434
00:37:53,221 --> 00:37:57,680
acetylene gas cylinders rocketing through the air

435
00:37:59,780 --> 00:38:04,720
the flying projectiles terrorized residents and destroyed property

436
00:38:04,721 --> 00:38:08,460
throughout a neighboring residential area

437
00:38:08,461 --> 00:38:22,080
Praxair repackaged industrial gases from large bulk containers into smaller containers that

438
00:38:22,081 --> 00:38:25,860
were used by the local industry in St. Louis.

439
00:38:25,861 --> 00:38:31,260
Vassily was handling about 30,000 cylinders, of which a vast majority were outside in the

440
00:38:31,261 --> 00:38:34,420
yard on an asphalt paved area.

441
00:38:34,421 --> 00:38:44,980
On the day of the disaster, St. Louis was in the midst of a sweltering heat wave.

442
00:38:45,660 --> 00:38:49,340
By mid-afternoon, temperatures neared the century mark,

443
00:38:49,780 --> 00:38:52,840
creating a volatile environment for pressurized propylene tanks.

444
00:38:55,020 --> 00:38:58,180
The gas is maintained a liquid under pressure,

445
00:38:58,460 --> 00:39:02,360
but as you add temperature to it, the pressure increases.

446
00:39:04,420 --> 00:39:10,840
Adding to the heat was Praxair's asphalt yard, which drove temperatures well above ambient inside the cylinders.

447
00:39:11,520 --> 00:39:18,860
The amplified pressure of hot expanding gas caused a safety pressure valve on a cylinder filled with propylene to open.

448
00:39:19,220 --> 00:39:24,260
This safety release device opened and allowed this highly flammable gas to vent.

449
00:39:25,160 --> 00:39:29,180
As the gas vented, the gas actually cools due to expansion,

450
00:39:29,181 --> 00:39:34,860
and little droplets of liquid form as the gas is venting,

451
00:39:34,940 --> 00:39:36,780
and these create a static charge,

452
00:39:37,000 --> 00:39:41,180
which allowed a self-ignition to occur, igniting the cylinder.

453
00:39:42,840 --> 00:39:45,560
The fire quickly spread to adjacent cylinders,

454
00:39:46,080 --> 00:39:49,760
which became superheated and leaked additional flammable gas.

455
00:39:54,280 --> 00:39:56,680
A chain reaction of explosions

456
00:39:56,681 --> 00:39:59,480
startled residents in the surrounding Lafayette district.

457
00:40:00,100 --> 00:40:02,420
It just got bigger and louder to the point that we were like,

458
00:40:02,480 --> 00:40:05,600
something's wrong, and you could just see these flames

459
00:40:05,601 --> 00:40:07,200
that look like a mushroom cloud,

460
00:40:07,380 --> 00:40:11,680
I mean, literally hundreds of feet tall with shrapnel coming out of them.

461
00:40:19,720 --> 00:40:22,960
An emergency dispatch reached the St. Louis Fire Department,

462
00:40:23,160 --> 00:40:24,180
which quickly responded.

463
00:40:26,680 --> 00:40:31,920
when we came up on the scene I would describe it as a war zone we're seeing

464
00:40:31,921 --> 00:40:35,140
these pieces of metal in the street initially we don't know what they are

465
00:40:35,141 --> 00:40:38,120
but then they start falling down in front of us and we realized that these

466
00:40:38,121 --> 00:40:46,840
are these tanks hundreds of tanks shot through the air crashing through brick

467
00:40:46,841 --> 00:40:52,600
walls and setting cars on fire the chaotic nature of the blaze forced

468
00:40:52,601 --> 00:40:57,220
firefighters to take a step back. To protect themselves, they stationed

469
00:40:57,221 --> 00:41:01,560
unmanned hoses on towers to douse the flames.

470
00:41:03,100 --> 00:41:07,380
They then shut down the highway, canceling that evening's baseball game

471
00:41:07,381 --> 00:41:14,100
at Busch Stadium, and began evacuating the neighborhood. It took several hours

472
00:41:14,101 --> 00:41:21,220
for firefighters to extinguish the raging inferno. Luckily, no one was killed and

473
00:41:21,221 --> 00:41:23,440
and only a handful of residents were injured.

474
00:41:24,520 --> 00:41:25,580
In the days that followed,

475
00:41:26,240 --> 00:41:28,520
officials from the United States Chemical Safety Board

476
00:41:28,521 --> 00:41:30,420
began investigating the cause of the fire.

477
00:41:31,920 --> 00:41:34,520
They discovered that the valve on the leaking cylinder

478
00:41:34,521 --> 00:41:35,940
prematurely opened,

479
00:41:35,941 --> 00:41:38,120
a result of weak design standards

480
00:41:38,121 --> 00:41:40,180
associated with many gas cylinder valves.

481
00:41:41,600 --> 00:41:45,080
The valve operates by a spring-loaded mechanism,

482
00:41:45,320 --> 00:41:47,320
and there's a small disk inside the valve

483
00:41:47,321 --> 00:41:49,300
which covers a gas port,

484
00:41:49,301 --> 00:41:54,940
And as the pressure builds up under that disk, it compresses the spring and it opens.

485
00:41:55,380 --> 00:41:59,260
And the spring rate establishes the setting of the valve.

486
00:41:59,580 --> 00:42:04,380
The particular valves that are used on these cylinders tend to have a lot of variability.

487
00:42:06,560 --> 00:42:10,380
Praxair has subsequently replaced all the relief valves on their propylene cylinders.

488
00:42:10,381 --> 00:42:17,520
approximately one year after the fire

489
00:42:17,521 --> 00:42:21,280
Praxair announced plans to reopen at St. Louis facility

490
00:42:21,281 --> 00:42:25,260
residents in the Lafayette district were outraged

491
00:42:25,261 --> 00:42:29,220
as some were still working to repair the damage caused by the flying cylinders

492
00:42:29,221 --> 00:42:33,400
basically what it came down to is that it is incompatible

493
00:42:33,401 --> 00:42:36,380
to have a residential neighborhood

494
00:42:36,381 --> 00:42:40,560
and that type of industrial business right on top of each other.

495
00:42:40,660 --> 00:42:42,720
It came down to safety, pure and simple.

496
00:42:43,520 --> 00:42:45,360
The concerned residents got their wish.

497
00:42:46,280 --> 00:42:49,160
Soon after their announcement, Praxair changed its mind.

498
00:42:50,060 --> 00:42:52,900
It relocated its St. Louis facility to Illinois

499
00:42:52,901 --> 00:42:55,140
in a non-residential area.

